## Phase 1: Indexing Pipeline

When you click "Index" on a video, this is what happens frame by frame:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    V["📹 Video File"] --> EXT["Extract Frames<br/>(seek at 1 FPS)"]
    EXT --> HASH["Perceptual Hash<br/>(16×16 grayscale)"]
    HASH -->|"Similar to prev?"| SKIP["⏭️ Skip frame"]
    HASH -->|"New scene"| SAVE["💾 Save frame JPEG<br/>(background thread)"]
    SAVE --> YOLO["🔍 YOLO11n Detection<br/>(find all objects)"]
    YOLO --> CROP["✂️ Crop each object<br/>+ save JPEG"]
    CROP --> BATCH["📦 Add to Mega-Batch<br/>(accumulate 32 frames)"]
    BATCH -->|"Batch full"| CLIPENC["🧠 CLIP Encode ALL images<br/>(1 bulk call for 32 frames<br/>+ all their crops)"]
    CLIPENC --> FAISS["📊 Add vectors to FAISS"]
    FAISS --> DBINS["🗄️ Batch insert to SQLite<br/>(frames + detections + embeddings)"]
    DBINS --> NEXT["Next frame..."]
```

### What Each Step Does

#### 1. Frame Extraction ([extract_frames](file:///D:/cpc/vision_search/indexer.py#80-105))
- Opens the video with OpenCV
- **Seeks directly** to every Nth frame (e.g., at 30fps video with 1 FPS extraction, it jumps every 30 frames)
- Yields [(frame_number, timestamp, frame_bgr)](file:///D:/cpc/vision_search/app.py#82-87)

#### 2. Scene Change Detection ([_frame_hash](file:///D:/cpc/vision_search/indexer.py#48-53) + [_frames_similar](file:///D:/cpc/vision_search/indexer.py#55-60))
- Resizes frame to tiny 16×16 grayscale → flattens to 256-element vector
- Computes **cosine similarity** with previous frame's hash
- If similarity > 0.92 → **skip** (nothing changed in the scene)
- Saves ~5-10% of redundant frames on typical videos

#### 3. YOLO11n Object Detection (`models.detect_objects`)
- Runs YOLOv11-nano on the full frame
- Detects all 80 COCO classes (people, cars, bags, phones, etc.) at confidence ≥ 0.3
- Returns bounding boxes + class names + confidence scores
- **Example output**: `[{class: "person", bbox: (100,200,300,500), conf: 0.92}, {class: "backpack", ...}]`

#### 4. Crop & Save
- Each detected object is cropped from the frame with 10% padding
- Saved as individual JPEG files (e.g., `frame_000030_det_0.jpg`)
- Disk writes happen in **background threads** (non-blocking)

#### 5. CLIP Encoding (`models.encode_images_clip`)
This is the **heart of the system**. CLIP (Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training) creates a **768-dimensional vector** for each image that lives in a shared embedding space with text.

**Key insight**: If you encode the text "red bag" and an image of a red bag, their vectors will be **close together** (high cosine similarity).

- Accumulates images across 32 frames into a **mega-batch**
- One batch might contain: 32 full frames + ~100 crop images = ~132 images
- All encoded in a single bulk CLIP call (much faster than 132 individual calls)
- Each image → 768-dim normalized float32 vector

#### 6. FAISS Index
- Facebook AI Similarity Search — an in-memory vector index
- Uses **Inner Product (IP)** similarity (equivalent to cosine similarity for normalized vectors)
- Each CLIP vector gets a sequential FAISS ID
- Stored to disk as `index.faiss`

#### 7. SQLite Database
Stores the metadata linking FAISS IDs back to actual files:

```
videos → frames → detections (with faiss_id, bbox, class_name)
                → frame_embeddings (with faiss_id for full-frame vectors)
```

### What Gets Stored Per Frame

For a single frame with 3 detected objects:

| Item | FAISS Vector | SQLite Row | JPEG File |
|------|-------------|------------|-----------|
| Full frame | ✅ (768-dim) | [frame_embeddings](file:///D:/cpc/vision_search/database.py#198-204) | `frame_000030.jpg` |
| Crop: person | ✅ (768-dim) | [detections](file:///D:/cpc/vision_search/database.py#206-215) | `frame_000030_det_0.jpg` |
| Crop: backpack | ✅ (768-dim) | [detections](file:///D:/cpc/vision_search/database.py#206-215) | `frame_000030_det_1.jpg` |
| Crop: car | ✅ (768-dim) | [detections](file:///D:/cpc/vision_search/database.py#206-215) | `frame_000030_det_2.jpg` |

**Total CLIP vectors per frame**: 1 (frame) + N (objects) = typically 1-5 vectors

---

## Phase 2: Search Pipeline

When you type a query like *"person wearing red jacket"*:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Q["🔤 User Query:<br/>'person wearing red jacket'"] --> PARSE["Parse Query<br/>(negative prompts, attributes)"]
    PARSE --> BLEND["Attribute Blending<br/>60% full query + 40% 'red'"]
    BLEND --> CLIPT["🧠 CLIP Text Encode<br/>→ 768-dim query vector"]
    CLIPT --> S1["⚡ Stage 1: FAISS Search<br/>Find top 100 nearest vectors<br/>(< 1ms)"]
    S1 --> LOOKUP["🗄️ SQLite Lookup<br/>Map FAISS IDs → frame paths"]
    LOOKUP --> S2["🔍 Stage 2: YOLO-World<br/>Re-rank top 20 candidates<br/>(~1-2 sec)"]
    S2 --> TEMPORAL["📈 Temporal Boost<br/>Boost clustered frames,<br/>penalize isolated ones"]
    TEMPORAL --> RESULTS["📋 Final Results<br/>(ranked by combined score)"]
```

### Stage 1: CLIP Vector Recall (instant, < 1ms)

1. **Query Parsing**: Handles special syntax:
   - `"red bag --no blue"` → subtracts "blue" vector from "red bag" vector
   - `"red jacket"` → blends: 60% "red jacket" + 40% "red" (emphasizes color)

2. **CLIP Text Encoding**: Converts query text → 768-dim vector (same space as image vectors)

3. **FAISS Search**: Finds the top ~100 vectors closest to the query vector
   - This searches ALL vectors — both full-frame AND individual crop vectors
   - Sub-millisecond even with millions of vectors

### Stage 2: YOLO-World Re-ranking (~1-2 seconds)

YOLO-World is an **open-vocabulary** object detector. Unlike regular YOLO (fixed 80 classes), it can detect *anything* you describe in text.

1. Sets the detection class to the user's exact query: `["person wearing red jacket"]`
2. Runs YOLO-World on each of the top 20 candidate frames
3. **Scoring**:
   - If YOLO-World finds the object → **boost score** (+80% of detection confidence)
   - If YOLO-World finds nothing → **heavy penalty** (score × 0.1)
4. Re-sorts results by updated score

**Why this matters**: CLIP might return frame #42 as top-1 because it vaguely looks like a jacket scene. But YOLO-World actually *looks for* a red jacket in that frame and confirms (or denies) its presence.

### Stage 2.5: Temporal Continuity Boost

Real objects persist across frames. If a result appears in a cluster of nearby timestamps (within 3 seconds), it gets a 20% boost. Isolated single-frame results get a 10% penalty.

### (Optional) Stage 3: VLM Enrichment

Not currently active during indexing, but can be used at search time to generate rich text descriptions using Ollama VLM.

---

## Why CLIP Encodes Both Frames AND Crops

```
 Search: "red backpack"
 
 Full Frame CLIP Vector:                    Crop CLIP Vector:
 ┌──────────────────────┐                   ┌──────────┐
 │  🧑 person           │                   │ 🎒       │
 │  🎒 red backpack     │  ← captures       │ red      │  ← captures
 │  🚗 car in bg        │    everything      │ backpack │    ONLY the
 │  🌳 trees            │    (noisy)         │          │    backpack
 │  ☁️  sky              │                   └──────────┘    (precise)
 └──────────────────────┘
 
 CLIP similarity to "red backpack":
   Full frame: 0.25 (weak - too much noise)
   Crop:       0.78 (strong - exact match!)
```

**Full-frame vectors** capture the overall scene — good for queries like "outdoor parking lot" or "crowded room".

**Crop vectors** capture individual objects — good for queries like "red backpack" or "person in white shirt".

By indexing **both**, the system handles both types of queries well.

---

## File Structure

```
vision_search/
├── app.py          → FastAPI server, routes, startup
├── config.py       → All settings (models, paths, thresholds)
├── models.py       → AI model loading & inference (CLIP, YOLO, VLM)
├── indexer.py      → Video indexing pipeline
├── searcher.py     → 3-stage search pipeline
├── database.py     → SQLite schema & CRUD operations
├── static/         → Web UI (HTML/CSS/JS)
└── data/
    ├── vision_search.db   → SQLite database
    ├── index.faiss        → CLIP vector index
    └── frames/
        └── {video_id}/    → Extracted frame & crop JPEGs
```

---

## Current Performance Bottleneck

For your 1-minute video (0.65 frames/s on CPU):

| Step | Time per Frame | % of Total |
|------|---------------|------------|
| CLIP encode (frame + ~3 crops) | **~1.2s** | **~78%** |
| YOLO detection | ~0.2s | ~13% |
| Frame extraction + hashing | ~0.1s | ~6% |
| Disk I/O + DB | ~0.05s | ~3% |

**CLIP encoding is the dominant bottleneck** — it processes the full frame PLUS every detected crop. Skipping CLIP on crops would cut encoding work by ~3-4x.
